G1 Garbage Collector (GB - TB)
    - Heap divided into fixed sized regions
    - Continuously monitors region usage
    - Selects regions with most garbage to reclaim
    - Evacuation, copies live objects into other regions
    - References updated

    - Pause Time Goal, aims to keep pause times predictable and within user defined goal
    - Most of the work done concurrently with threads

Important JVM options
    - XX:+UseG1GC
    - XX:MacGCPauseMillis=<ms>
    - XX:InitiatingHeap

Sample run: java -XX:+UseG1GC -Xms16m -Xmx64m -Xlog:gc GcObserve
    - -XX:+UseG1GC -- Specifies to use G1 GC
    - -Xms16m -- Initial Heap around 16 MB
    - -Xmx64m -- Maximum Heap around 64 MB
    - -Xlog:gc -- Print GC Events



ZGC (Z Garbage Collector) (TB)
    - Ultra-low pause, scalable garbage collector for large heaps (TB Scale)
    - 1-2ms pause times, ultra low pause // super low latency
    - Cloud-native and containerized, microservices, real-time analytics, trading systems
    - Trade off - Higher CPU overhead and more metadata memory


java -XX:+UseZGC -Xms16m -Xmx64m -Xlog:gc FileToRun
java -XX:+UseZGC -XX:+PrintCommandLineFlags -version


Memory Leaks
    - Objects no longer in use are still referenced, exists in heap but no reference in stack
    - Increased memory usage, degrade performance, OutOfMemoryError (OOM)
    - Ideal solution: delete object and then make null, if you make null first it will be unreachable = memory leak

    - Detection Tools (VisualVM, Eclipse MAT, JProfiler / YourKit, GC Logs (-Xlog:gc*)

    - Close resources in finally block or try-with-resources

